   "title": "Ex-INEC Commissioner Urges Trump To Tie US Policy To Credible 2027 Polls",
   "url": "https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/11/15/ex-inec-commissioner-urges-trump-to-tie-us-policy-to-credible-2027-polls/",
   "domain": "thisdaylive.com",
   "source": "ThisDay（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Sun, 15 Nov 2026 16:57:44 +0000",
   "_dt": 1794761864.0,
   "ageDays": 0.04,
   "summary": "The former INEC national commissioner argued that Nigerians are not leaving Nigeria because they do not love their country, but because decades of electoral malpractice, corruption, insecurity and weak accountability.",
   "summary_zh": "独立国家选举委员会（INEC）前全国委员认为，尼日利亚人离开尼日利亚并非因为他们不热爱自己的国家，而是因为数十年来存在的选举舞弊、腐败、安全局势不佳以及问责机制薄弱。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "In the letter dated November 15, 2026, Igini said the US government should make the announcement before the election and not after. The former INEC national commissioner argued that Nigerians are not leaving Nigeria because they do not love their country, but because decades of electoral malpractice, corruption, insecurity and weak accountability. This, he noted, has destroyed confidence in government and forced thousands of professionals, students and families to seek refuge in the United States. Igini added: “Border enforcement alone cannot solve this. We must address the cause at the source. “Nigeria has the population, oil, gas and talent to be one of the richest nations on earth. But [it has been] undermined by [a] leadership recruitment process that has rendered the country a plundered wilderness, littered with the debris of missed opportunities.” In consultation with European partners, igini stressed that the U.S should issue a clear warning to the INEC Chairman, national commissioners, resident electoral commissioners and ad hoc staff drawn from federal agencies and universities. He said officials who engage in deliberate manipulation of results, falsification of electoral records or other serious electoral offences established by credible evidence should face individual consequences, including visa restrictions and, where legally justified, targeted financial sanctions and asset freezes. Igini also proposed extending consequences to the immediate family members where applicable. While stressing that judicial independence is indispensable, Igini said it must not become a shield for deliberate corruption or subversion of electoral justice. He maintained that judicial officers whose deliberate corruption or other sanctionable misconduct is established by credible evidence should face visa restrictions and asset seizures. Igini stressed that the Nigeria Police Force, Department of State Services (DSS), armed forces, Nigeria Security and Civil Defence Corps (NSCDC) and other security institutions have a duty to protect citizens and obey the law impartially, not politicians, and to stem growing violence and thuggery since campaigns commenced. He emphasised that senior officers and commanders whose authorisation or facilitation of voter intimidation, electoral violence, unlawful suppression of opposition or manipulation of outcomes is established by credible evidence should face visa restrictions and other sanctions. He noted that the police and DSS are currently headed by two officers with enviable records of service. Igini maintained that the US should support credible domestic and international observers to monitor the process before, during and after the election, with particular attention to voter intimidation, impartial conduct of security personnel, publication of polling-unit results, transparent collation and resolution of electoral disputes. Igini argued that the doctrine serves Trump’s America-First agenda in four ways: Reduces migration pressure as credible elections produce good leaders. He explained that good leaders fix the economy, saying when Nigeria works, Nigerians will stay home and many in the US diaspora will return. Igini said it would also help the US to stop legitimising and funding governments that lack the consent of their people. Founded on January 22, 1995, THISDAY is published by THISDAY NEWSPAPERS LTD., 35 Creek Road Apapa, Lagos, Nigeria with offices in 36 states of Nigeria , the Federal Capital Territory and around the world. It is Nigeria’s most authoritative news media available on all platforms for the political, business, professional and diplomatic elite and broader middle classes while serving as the meeting point of new ideas, culture and technology for the aspirationals and millennials. The newspaper is a public trust dedicated to the pursuit of truth and reason covering a range of issues from breaking news to politics, business, the markets, the arts, sports and community to the crossroads of people and society. You can email us at: hello@thisdaylive.com or visit our contact us page",
