   "title": "India should become innovator, global leader in advanced tech: Rajnath Singh",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/india-should-become-innovator-global-leader-in-advanced-tech-rajnath-singh/article71705038.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Sun, 15 Nov 2026 19:53:34 +0530",
   "_dt": 1794752614.0,
   "ageDays": 0.15,
   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription Defence Minister Rajnath Singh on Sunday (November 15, 2026) called for strengthening India’s indigenous Non-Destructive Testing (NDT) capabilities, saying the country must move beyond being a user of advanced technologies to become a developer, innovator and global leader in critical technological domains.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅以解锁这些内容 国防部长拉杰纳特·辛格于周日（2026年11月15日）呼吁加强印度本土无损检测（NDT）能力，称印度必须超越先进技术使用国的阶段，成为关键技术领域的开发者、创新者和全球领导者。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Defence Minister Rajnath Singh on Sunday (November 15, 2026) called for strengthening India’s indigenous Non-Destructive Testing (NDT) capabilities, saying the country must move beyond being a user of advanced technologies to become a developer, innovator and global leader in critical technological domains. “There can be no compromise on the integrity of testing results in strategic applications,” Mr. Singh said, stressing the need to develop indigenous capabilities in advanced inspection technologies, specialised sensors, evaluation equipment, and associated software to reduce technological dependence and secure critical defence supply chains. He said NDT technologies enable the examination of materials, components, and structures without causing damage, much like ultrasound and X-rays help examine the human body without surgical intervention. With Indian defence systems becoming increasingly sophisticated, every critical material, component, and manufacturing process must meet the highest standards of quality and reliability, he said. Hidden defects could affect operational availability, increase maintenance requirements, reduce equipment service life, and, in critical situations, jeopardise mission success. “We need to move beyond a culture of merely identifying defects towards actively preventing them by embedding quality at every stage of the manufacturing process,” he said. Underlining the strategic significance of NDT, Mr. Singh said quality in defence applications, including missiles, aircraft, naval platforms, and spacecraft, was not merely a manufacturing requirement but an essential element of operational reliability and national security. He cited India’s achievements in missile development, naval platforms, aerospace engineering, radar systems, and space applications as evidence of its expanding indigenous capabilities. The commissioning of the country’s first indigenous aircraft carrier, INS Vikrant , was an important milestone in India’s journey towards self-reliance, he said. The Defence Minister also highlighted the growing role of artificial intelligence, machine learning, robotics, advanced sensors, digital twins, and predictive analytics in improving inspection accuracy and structural health monitoring. He called for developing indigenous solutions suited to India’s strategic and industrial requirements. Referring to initiatives such as Innovations for Defence Excellence (iDEX) and the Technology Development Fund (TDF), he said collaboration among the government, industry, academia, and research institutions was crucial to technological advancement. The three-day conference has brought together over 2,000 professionals, researchers, industry experts, and students to discuss emerging NDT technologies and their applications, particularly in defence and aerospace. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "in"
   ],
   "topic": [
    "sec",
    "pol"
   ],
   "tier": 4,
   "actor": "第三方",
   "alsoUS": false,
   "cnHit": false,
