   "summary": "Share Comments Add Euronews on Google Share Facebook Twitter Flipboard Send Reddit Linkedin Messenger Telegram VK Bluesky Threads Whatsapp Putin has indicated that Russia will not back down on territorial demands to control parts of Ukraine and has rejected the idea of foreign peacekeeping troops being deployed in the country. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said Washington had proposed the idea of Ukraine ceding land to Russia in exchange for NATO membership as efforts to bring an end to the war continue.",
   "summary_zh": "分享 评论 添加 Euronews于Google分享 Facebook Twitter Flipboard 发送 Reddit Linkedin Messenger Telegram VK Bluesky Threads Whatsapp 普京表示，俄罗斯不会在领土要求上作出让步，仍将坚持控制乌克兰部分地区的目标，并拒绝向该国派遣外国维和部队。乌克兰总统沃洛迪米尔·泽连斯基说，随着结束战争的努力持续推进，美国提出了让乌克兰割让土地给俄罗斯，以换取加入北约的方案。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Share Comments Add Euronews on Google Share Facebook Twitter Flipboard Send Reddit Linkedin Messenger Telegram VK Bluesky Threads Whatsapp Putin has indicated that Russia will not back down on territorial demands to control parts of Ukraine and has rejected the idea of foreign peacekeeping troops being deployed in the country. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said Washington had proposed the idea of Ukraine ceding land to Russia in exchange for NATO membership as efforts to bring an end to the war continue. \"The Americans raised a question: 'What if Ukraine joins NATO? Would Ukraine withdraw from Donbas?' This was the first time this had been raised,\" Zelenskyy told reporters on Friday in comments embargoed until Saturday, the AFP news agency reported. Zelenskyy added that Ukraine was discussing \"specific security guarantees\" from NATO and the European Union and that he would ask Ukrainians if they accepted the terms should Western nations confirm. It comes after Russian President Vladimir Putin met with US special envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner in Moscow earlier this week. During their meeting, Putin signalled that Russia would not back down on territorial demands to control parts of Ukraine and rejected the idea of foreign peacekeeping troops being deployed in the country. The US envoys later met with Zelenskyy in Ireland on Thursday to discuss ongoing diplomatic efforts, with the trio announcing that, moving forward, Europe would have a \"voice\" in peace negotiations. \"This will allow us to address the broader postwar European security arrangements alongside the territorial issues between Ukraine and Russia,\" Kushner wrote in a post on X. \"They are striving by every means possible to join the negotiations, but, as our President has said, they must decide whether they want peace or the continuation of the war,\" he said",
